## Title and meta formula
Front-load the primary keyword. Title 50–60 characters. Meta 120–155 characters with the actual answer in the first ~120.

| Device | Use when | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Brackets/parentheses suffix | Default | Bracketed clarification measured at +38% CTR in a 3.3M-headline study |
| Number | Only if structurally true of the body | A number the body doesn't deliver is a broken promise; count the real items first |
| Question | Only if the GSC/forum query is question-shaped | Matches how people search |
| Colon + subtitle | Hook + payoff | ~9% lift in one test |
| "Who"-framing | Only with a real named authority on the page | +22% vs −37% for "why" |
| Year/season tag | Time-sensitive topics; prefer the current season + year when the refresh is seasonal | 15–25% lift while fresh; a stale year hurts trust |
| Power word | One at most, never stacked | Stacking reads as spam |

**Rules**
- **Maximum 2 devices per title.** A year inside a bracket counts as one device.
- Never double quotation marks in title/meta (Google truncates at the mark). No ending punctuation, except a genuine question title.
- Never promise what the body does not deliver. A number goes in the title only if true; if only a new section can carry a true count, put it in that section's H2 and the meta instead.
- If the page earns impressions on head terms it only partly matches ("nail art designs"), absorb them with wording (C1), but never shift away from the page's primary-keyword intent.
- American English; no banned vocabulary.
- Pick **one** title and one meta. Mention a runner-up only on a genuine close call, in one line.

